{
  "meta": {
    "note": "Keyframe poses for the stick figures. Joints are three-dimensional and drawn in code with Compose Canvas; no image files and no third-party animation assets, so everything here works offline and can ship in a public repo.",
    "coordinate_space": "Every joint is [x, y, z], centred on the figure and roughly between minus a half and plus a half. x runs left to right, y runs up to down, so a larger y is lower. z runs away from the viewer to toward the viewer.",
    "camera": "Each exercise names its own camera in degrees. Azimuth 0 looks straight down the z axis, so you see the x-y plane; azimuth 90 looks along the x axis instead. Elevation lifts the viewer above eye level. Because the poses are three-dimensional, changing the camera shows the same movement from a different angle rather than needing new poses.",
    "symmetry": "mirror_z reflects the written-down arm and leg across z, for exercises laid out along x such as a push-up. mirror_x reflects across x, for exercises facing the camera such as a pull-up. none means the two sides genuinely differ and both are written out, as in a split squat.",
    "how_to_fix_a_pose": "Move the numbers, not the drawing code. Keep the distance between two joints about the same across every pose of one exercise, or the limb will appear to stretch as it moves. A test fails if it does.",
    "review_status": "push_up, pull_up and hollow_hold are the first three, built for approval before the other twelve."
  },

